Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Use regex to replace markdown tags with HTML tags
    std::regex regex(R"(^#.*?)\((.*?)(.*?)\)");
    std::string html;
    std::smatch match;

    while (std::regex_search(markdown, match, regex)) {
        std::string title = match[1];
        std::string content = match[2];
        html += "<h1>" + title + "</h1>" + content + "<br>";
    }

    return html;
}
```

This function uses regular expressions to replace markdown tags with HTML tags. The regular expression `^#.*?)\((.*?)(.*?)\)` matches markdown headings in the format `# title (content)`. The `std::regex_search` function is used to find all matches in the input string, and the matches are extracted using the `match` object. The title and content are then wrapped in HTML tags and appended to the `html` string.

Note that this function does not support other markdown elements such as lists, links, or code. You would need to modify the regular expression to support these elements.

